v32opt known issues -- post-bugfix state, Sep 23 2026
(supersedes the Sep 15 audit list. Everything marked FIXED below was
fixed, regression-tested against its minimal reproduction, and the
full unit suite (19/19) passes; see "REMAINING" for what is still
open. "RMW" = read-modify-write to memory, e.g. "IADD [R1+0], 5")

FIXED -- CONFIRMED MISCOMPILES (from the Sep 15 audit):

-fpeephole-immediate-prop
  FIXED: Pattern 2 no longer folds across reads of the register or
  across JT/JF branches (the rewritten MOV executes on every path
  that reaches it, including the branch-taken side).

-fpeephole-forwarding
  FIXED: Rule 1 now models every memory write (RMW ALU ops, aliasing
  stores through a different base register, MOVS/SETS/CMPS) and no
  longer forwards across a CALL (a callee can write arbitrary
  memory through pointers).

-fpeephole-dead-stores
  FIXED: only a pure "MOV [loc], x" counts as an overwriter; an RMW
  ("IADD [BP-4],1") or "PUSH [loc]" READS the slot and blocks the
  match. MOVS/SETS/CMPS also recognized as memory traffic.

-fpeephole-loads, -fpeephole-compiler-myopia, -fpeephole-movs
  FIXED: same RMW / aliasing / MOVS-SETS modeling applied to each
  pass's memory-write and aliasing guards.

-fpeephole-jmp-chain
  FIXED: the intermediate JMP is only removed when nothing else can
  reach its label (no other branch targets it AND nothing can fall
  into it). Also consumes exactly one --trigger-max slot per
  transform (was two).

-fpeephole-shifts
  FIXED: the unsound rules are gone -- "SHL r,N; SHL r,-N" pairs
  (bits shifted out are lost; not an identity) and "SHL r,r"
  (shifts by r's own value) are both kept.

-fpeephole-pairs
  FIXED: NOT removed from the self-inverting list (NOT NOT x ==
  CIB(x), not x, for non-boolean x); nonexistent "NEG" dropped;
  XOR/self-inverse pairs match register destinations only (indirect
  destinations were compared by base register alone, ignoring the
  offset).

-fomit-frame-pointers
  FIXED: any [BP+N]/[BP-N] reference now blocks elimination (it is
  BP-relative addressing and needs the frame). Also fixed: bodies
  that merely READ SP ("MOV R1, SP") block elimination (SP sits 2
  words higher without the prologue); CALL is exempt from the SP
  check (net-zero across a call under both supported compilers).

-fcse
  FIXED: source operands are now tracked between the two matching
  expressions -- a modified source register, or a memory write to a
  memory-based source, blocks the replacement.

-finline
  FIXED: body collection stops at function/data boundary labels and
  at HLT (a noreturn body used to swallow the following function and
  any data directives); any direct BP reference (read OR write) and
  [BP+0]/[BP+1] (saved-BP / return-address slots) disqualify a
  candidate; -finline-max is clamped to 0..32 (unclamped values
  overflowed the candidate body arrays straight off the stack).

-promote-leaf / -promote-regs / -promote-loops
  FIXED: "MOV R1, BP" (capturing the frame address) now blocks
  promotion (the old address-taken check excluded ALL MOVs); the
  stack-slot flush before RET is now inserted before the epilogue
  ("MOV SP, BP"), not between "POP BP" and RET where [BP-N] would
  have addressed the CALLER's frame.

FIXED -- DEAD CODE / TOOLING:

-fpeephole-immediates
  The unreachable float branch was REMOVED (FP addition is not
  associative: "(x+a)-b != x+(a-b)" and "(x+a)-a != x"; only
  bit-exact identities may touch float arithmetic).

-fpeephole-reduce
  Float identities are now reachable: FMUL/FDIV by 1.0 are removed
  (exact for all x); "FMUL r, 0.0" is deliberately NOT rewritten
  (Inf/NaN * 0.0 = NaN; -x * 0.0 = -0.0).

--dot + any node-removing pass
  FIXED: the DOT export always rebuilds the CFG from the FINAL
  instruction list (the old export walked the stale pre-cleanup CFG,
  i.e. freed nodes).

general
  CALL/RET now count as moving SP in modifies_register() (hardware
  return-address push/pop) for every pass that asks. main() frees
  the whole instruction list, not just the head node. CFG: runs of
  up to 16 consecutive labels are attached (was 8, extras silently
  dropped -> missing edges -> unsound constant propagation); labels
  at end-of-file get a trailing block; indented labels no longer
  break jump-target matching (label text is trimmed).

REMAINING (known, open):

- peephole-jumps pattern 3 (unreachable-code elimination) does not
  reason about computed jump targets: "JMP R0" / "JMP 0x1000" leaves
  the following code alone (conservative; deliberate).

- dce's function-boundary heuristic treats any "__function_*_return:"
  label as internal, so a REAL function whose name ends in "_return"
  (e.g. Lua "function get_return()") is absorbed into the preceding
  function. Needs a stronger naming contract from the compilers.

- constant-folding trades register operands for immediates
  ("MOV Rd, Rs" -> "MOV Rd, const" is 2 words instead of 1), so the
  assembled .vbin can GROW; its payoff is enabling further
  immediate-based peepholes, not raw size. (Observed: +10% vbin on
  the constant-folding fixture.) A post-fold "immediate -> register
  re-materialization" pass would fix this.

- trigger accounting granularity varies by pass: most passes count
  one slot per deleted node, omit-frame-pointers one per 4-node
  group, dce one per swept function, promote-* one per promoted
  slot. --trigger-max bisection is consistent within a pass but not
  across passes.

- the promote-* passes remain experimental (opt-in only) and are
  not covered by the unit suite except promote-loops.

UNIT TESTS: 19/19 pass (make -C testing). Fixtures updated where
they encoded the old unsound expectations (shifts cancel pairs,
SHL r,r, NOT-NOT, FMUL 0.0, [BP-N] frame elimination, CIB-before-
JT/JF carve-out, immediate propagation in peephole-forwarding,
absolute-address unreachable code) or were misbuilt (constant-
floating: reserved word "skip" and undefined call target; scenarios
in cse/peephole-jumps now label-separated so one scenario's dead
zone cannot legitimately eat the next).

DIRECTIVE SAFETY (assembler directives are never deleted, moved past,
or duplicated):

- remove_node() refuses to delete any directive node (%define,
  %include, integer/float/string/pointer/datafile). It is the single
  choke point for every deleting pass, so this covers all of them.
  Origin: peephole-jumps pattern 3 deleted celeste.asm's PICO8_*
  %defines as "unreachable code" after a JMP.
- skip_other_nodes() no longer sees through DATA directives (it still
  skips comments, blanks and %-preprocessor lines, which emit nothing),
  so adjacency patterns cannot match across embedded data.
- inline: a body containing a directive is never a candidate, and a
  call site is declined if the body uses a %define that is defined
  below that call site (defines.c: define_visible_at()).
- dce: a label followed by data is a range boundary and its segment is
  always reachable, so a data table is never swept with the function
  textually before it; code after the data is still scanned.
- dce relies on Lua-mode naming: "__<stem>_skip:" continuations and
  "__global_*" helper labels (allowlist + branch-target backstop).

%define VALUE RESOLUTION (20261002-dev):

- An operand naming an integer %define carries its real value
  (defines.c: define_int_value(); filled in by
  defines_resolve_operands() after parsing and by parse_operand() for
  nodes created later). is_numeric_immediate() and cfg.c's
  is_symbolic_immediate() accept such operands, so peephole-algebra,
  -immediates, -immediate-prop, -reduce, -shifts and constant-folding
  fold through them. -fno-resolve-defines restores opaque symbols.
- Never resolved: floats, expressions, symbols redefined with a
  different value, and in Lua mode the NaN-boxing tags (BOXED_*,
  NAN_VALUE, CLOSURE_ADDR_MASK, any value with the 0x7F800000 pattern)
  -- cse/constant-folding recognise boxing idioms by NAME.
- A folded instruction is emitted with the computed number; the symbol
  name is lost at that site (readability only).
- Measured: celeste -8 instructions, nostalgick -1. Small on Lua-mode
  output, where >90% of %define uses are boxing tag checks.
- FIXED alongside: peephole-immediate-prop pattern 2 resumed at a saved
  'next' pointer that was the node it had just freed when the MOV and
  the folded ALU instruction were adjacent (use-after-free; latent
  until resolution made such adjacent pairs foldable).

PEEPHOLE-ZERO-TEST (new pass, 20261002-dev; on from -O1):

- "MOV Ra,Rb / IEQ|INE Ra,0 / JT|JF Ra,L" -> "JF|JT Rb,L", and the
  in-place form without the MOV. Requires Ra dead after the jump on
  both sides, proven by liveness.c (reg_dead_from/reg_dead_at_label):
  follows JMP/JT/JF, summarises callees (reads-before-write / always
  overwrites / may pass through), RET uses is_live_out_register().
  Every unknown answers "live". R0..R10 only.
- Measured: celeste 44421 -> 42953 instructions (-3.3%), nostalgick
  54260 -> 52506 (-3.2%). All converted sites re-proven by an
  independent checker.
- Counts removed instructions (2 for the copy form, 1 in place); one
  --trigger-max slot per site.
- The rewritten jump loses its trailing source comment.
- liveness.c is reusable; candidates: "MOV Ra,[mem] / MOV Rb,Ra" ->
  "MOV Rb,[mem]" when Ra is dead, and letting is_register_read()
  treat POP/IN destinations as pure writes for the other passes.
- Fixture: testing/peephole-zero-test (13 matches expected).

